## Nexus Industries and Toyota Announce Solid-State Battery Joint Venture

### $312 Million Investment to Accelerate Electric Vehicle Battery Technology

**PHOENIX, AZ and PLANO, TX – March 5, 2026** – Nexus Industries, Inc. (NYSE: NEXS) and Toyota Motor Corporation (NYSE: TM) today announced the formation of NextGen Battery Technologies LLC, a joint venture to develop and manufacture solid-state batteries for electric vehicles.

### Joint Venture Highlights

| Element | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| Name | NextGen Battery Technologies LLC |
| Ownership | 51% Nexus Industries / 49% Toyota |
| Total Investment | $312 million |
| Location | San Antonio, Texas |
| Target Production | Q4 2027 |
| Initial Capacity | 2 GWh |

"This partnership combines Nexus's breakthrough solid electrolyte technology with Toyota's world-class manufacturing expertise," said Dr. Victoria Chen, CEO of Nexus Industries. "Together, we will accelerate the transition to electric vehicles by delivering batteries that are safer, charge faster, and last longer than today's lithium-ion technology."

"Toyota is committed to leading the next generation of electric vehicle technology," said Koji Sato, CEO of Toyota Motor Corporation. "Solid-state batteries represent a paradigm shift in energy storage, and we are excited to partner with Nexus to bring this technology to market."

### Technology Advantages

Solid-state batteries replace the liquid electrolyte in conventional lithium-ion batteries with a solid ceramic material, offering significant advantages:

| Metric | Solid-State | Current Li-Ion | Improvement |
|--------|-------------|----------------|-------------|
| Energy Density | 400 Wh/kg | 260 Wh/kg | +54% |
| Fast Charge (10-80%) | 15 minutes | 30 minutes | 2x faster |
| Cycle Life | 1,500 cycles | 1,000 cycles | +50% |
| Safety | Non-flammable | Flammable | Significant |
| Operating Temperature | -30°C to 60°C | -20°C to 45°C | Extended |

### Development Timeline

| Milestone | Target Date |
|-----------|-------------|
| Pilot Line Start | Q2 2026 |
| First Cells Produced | Q3 2026 |
| Qualification Complete | Q4 2026 |
| Commercial Production | Q4 2027 |
| Full Capacity (2 GWh) | Q4 2028 |

### Investment Details

Nexus Industries will contribute $159 million, including $79 million in intellectual property and $80 million in cash. Toyota will contribute $153 million in cash. The joint venture will be headquartered in San Antonio, Texas, adjacent to Toyota's existing manufacturing campus.

Dr. Robert Martinez, currently Nexus's BatteryTech Program Director, has been named CEO of NextGen Battery Technologies. Hiroshi Tanaka, Toyota's EVP of Battery Technology, will serve as COO.

### Toyota Offtake Agreement

Toyota has agreed to purchase a minimum of 60% of the joint venture's production for the first five years, with total contract value expected to exceed $2 billion. Toyota plans to integrate solid-state batteries into premium electric vehicles beginning in 2028.

### Regulatory Approvals

The transaction has received all required regulatory approvals, including clearance under the Hart-Scott-Rodino Antitrust Improvements Act.

